{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>A light you shall make for the ark.</b> This is the window that Noah opened when he sent the raven and the dove, and while it was still sealed he placed there a precious stone to illuminate for them inside. And so the words of our Sages are consistent, for some say it was a window, and some say a precious stone — for as it appears, it is a matter of uncertainty, and the testimony of the verse there is that it was the window. And according to its plain sense, <b>a light you shall make for the ark</b> — the language of יצהר, meaning oil; you shall make [it] for the needs of the ark to illuminate in it. And one should not interpret it as a window to illuminate in it and bring light inside, for the constellations did not serve [i.e., shine] explicitly all the days of the Flood." "<b>And to a cubit you shall finish it from above.</b> It refers to the light: finish the window from above, near the roof, [leaving] a cubit, so that the rains do not harm it, for the covering served as a screen over it. And צוהר and חלון are used in both masculine and feminine forms, as in ״חלוני שקופים אטומים״, and likewise many others." "<b>And the door of the ark in its side you shall place.</b> In one corner, and not in the middle of the broad face, because it catches all the weight of the water and most of the wind bears upon it. Another interpretation: <b>in its side</b> — in the upper third of the height of the ark." "<b>Lower, second, and third.</b> The thirty [i.e., three] are the upper ones, and they were for man and for all food."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {3 "The Hebrew reads שלשים ('thirty') instead of the expected שלשית ('a third') or simply שלש ('three'). The translation follows the text literally as written; the context clearly refers to the three stories."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:03:19.503027702Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}